findings.body:
`testBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders` was renamed to `verifyBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders`. XCTest only runs methods with a `test` prefix, so the Bookmarks→Reminders share check no longer executes anywhere. The PR description says this is a flakiness fix, not a removal, so the coverage loss is unintentional.

The rename also leaves `AccessibilityTestPlan.xctestplan` line 371 referencing `ShareLongPressTests/testBookmarksShareNormalWebsiteReminders()`, which no longer exists. That stale entry can produce a test-plan error or warning depending on the Xcode version.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/ShareLongPressTests.swift" lines="46-48"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the goal was to disable the flaky test, use `XCTSkip` or remove it from the plan explicitly. Renaming it hides the disablement.

::callout{type="info"}
The rename also conflicts with the plan at `AccessibilityTestPlan.xctestplan:371`, which lists the old name.

The new `OnboardingTests.tearDown` returns early when the test is `testSelectBottomPlacement` or the device is an iPad. That `return` also skips `app.terminate()` and `try await super.tearDown()`. `BaseTestCase.tearDown` (line 119) does the same two calls, so the app is left running into the next test and the base teardown is bypassed.

The condition is also broader than the comment suggests. `self.name.contains("testSelectBottomPlacement")` is true on iPhone too, where that test does run. So the theme reset is skipped for an iPhone test that is not iPad-specific.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="27-38"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The guard should only skip the theme switch, not the termination and super teardown.

The new line in `openNewTabAndValidateURLisPaste` uses `springboard.buttons["Allow Paste"]`. The existing code for the same system alert uses `springboard.alerts.buttons["Allow Paste"]` in `TodayWidgetTests.swift:490` and `:600`, and `ClipBoardTests.swift:45` uses `springboard.buttons`. Because the new call uses `tapIfExists`, a wrong locator is silently ignored. The failure then appears later as an unrelated `mozWaitForValueContains` failure.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/BaseTestCase.swift" lines="468-473"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/BaseTestCase.swift" lines="471-471"}
springboard.alerts.buttons["Allow Paste"].tapIfExists(timeout: 0.5)

`if iPad() { let shouldSkipTest = true; try XCTSkipIf(shouldSkipTest, ...) }` is the same as `throw XCTSkip(...)` inside the `if`. The local flag adds nothing. Moving the skip ahead of the Terms tap is correct, because the test now exits before any UI interaction on iPad.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="348-352"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/XCUITests/OnboardingTests.swift" lines="348-351"}
if iPad() {
    throw XCTSkip("Toolbar option not available for iPad")
}
